goldReference.description:
Marks an element as a quality exemplar: the standard other elements are held to, and the few-shot reference for later authoring.

EVIDENCED, NOT DECLARED. It was a bare boolean an author could set on their own work with no review recorded anywhere — the shape decision 8b removed from `bootstrapAuthority`, and the shape `bok-article` refuses by name when it says there is deliberately no `authoritative: true` field. Setting it true is permitted only where `reviews` can carry it, and the validator computes that rather than believing it:

  - the element names its `authoring.authors`;
  - EVERY attainable level, 1 through `levelCeiling`, is covered by a review of type `technical` or `assessment` whose disposition is `accepted` or `accepted-with-changes`. A reviewer who saw three rungs of a five-rung ladder has not made the top two exemplary;
  - each such review still matches the element as it stands, so a later rewrite lapses the claim until somebody reviews it again;
  - and no review counts if its reviewer is one of the authors, which is the no-self-signoff rule reaching the corpus.

Leaving it false is always permitted and silent, exactly as understating a provenance tier is: plenty of well-reviewed elements are not the teaching example, and being reviewed obliges nobody to nominate you.
